00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:16.160
he was Saddam's biographer within the intelligence community so that was another thing his star

00:49:16.160 --> 00:49:21.280
was attached to that yeah the only person I can't believe it and his story so we'll get

00:49:21.280 --> 00:49:26.080
into it but his story so odd because what ended up happening was he was out of the CIA for a

00:49:26.080 --> 00:49:32.160
while and reporters started coming after him because they were looking for evidence of a torture

00:49:32.160 --> 00:49:37.120
program and they thought that he was involved and he sort of inadvertently sort of confirms

00:49:37.120 --> 00:49:42.960
some things yeah and that and that ends up like sort of sucking him into this crazy situation

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:47.840
where he's he's prosecuted so it's it's a it's a little bit different from a traditional

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:53.360
whistleblower story but that's out to do it right yeah exactly but you know he he

00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:58.560
didn't back down right yeah he spoke out against and apparently they were like he you know they asked

00:49:58.560 --> 00:50:02.000
him at the CIA if he wanted to be

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:01.800
trained and enhanced interrogation,

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:03.240
which is what they call the torture stuff, right?

00:50:03.240 --> 00:50:04.080
Right, yeah.

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:05.520
And like everyone else was like, yeah, sure.

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:06.360
And he didn't.

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:08.400
So without any further ado,

00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:09.640
let's talk to John Kiriakou.

00:50:09.640 --> 00:50:10.980
John Kiriakou.

00:50:10.980 --> 00:50:12.200
Wow, look at that rhyme.

00:50:12.200 --> 00:50:13.040
I am.

00:50:14.920 --> 00:50:16.160
John, welcome to Useful Idiots.

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:17.000
I'm Matt Taibi.

00:50:17.000 --> 00:50:17.920
This is Katie Halper.

00:50:17.920 --> 00:50:20.320
And you are John Kiriakou, right?

00:50:20.320 --> 00:50:21.160
I am.

00:50:21.160 --> 00:50:22.000
It's a pleasure.

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:22.840
Thanks for having me.

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:23.900
Thanks for coming on.

00:50:23.900 --> 00:50:25.940
So really looking forward to speaking to you.

00:50:25.940 --> 00:50:27.480
There's a couple of things that are in the news

00:50:28.480 --> 00:50:30.200
that are kind of relevant to your story

00:50:30.200 --> 00:50:33.400
and your experience that I want to ask about.

00:50:33.400 --> 00:50:36.240
You might have heard recently Bernie Sanders came out

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:41.740
with a statement that he would end the practice

00:50:41.740 --> 00:50:45.720
of using the Espionage Act for people who talk to journalists.

00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:48.780
And that seemed directly related to what happened to you.

00:50:48.780 --> 00:50:51.280
Can you talk a little bit about just give us

00:50:51.280 --> 00:50:53.640
the background of what happened with you

00:50:53.640 --> 00:50:55.800
and how you came to be charged with the Espionage

00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:57.080
Act, even though that wasn't what

00:50:57.160 --> 00:50:58.400
you went to prison for?

00:50:58.400 --> 00:51:03.400
Yeah, in 2007, I gave a nationally televised interview

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:06.800
to Brian Ross, who was then with ABC News.

00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:09.380
And in that interview, I blew the whistle

00:51:09.380 --> 00:51:11.000
on the CIA's torture program

00:51:11.000 --> 00:51:13.600
and I called waterboarding torture.

00:51:13.600 --> 00:51:16.160
I said that I thought that it was illegal.

00:51:16.160 --> 00:51:19.760
The FBI began investigating me within 24 hours,

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:21.200
as you might imagine.

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:23.360
And they investigated me for a full year

00:51:23.360 --> 00:51:26.880
from December of 2007 to December of 2008.

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:28.960
And then they issued to my attorneys

00:51:28.960 --> 00:51:30.840
something called a declination letter,

00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:33.640
saying that they were declining to prosecute.

00:51:33.640 --> 00:51:35.200
I thought the case was closed,

00:51:35.200 --> 00:51:37.360
actually took my wife out to dinner that night

00:51:37.360 --> 00:51:40.880
to celebrate, and what I didn't know

00:51:40.880 --> 00:51:42.400
was that four weeks later

00:51:42.400 --> 00:51:45.680
when Barack Obama was inaugurated,

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:49.200
John Brennan, an old nemesis of mine from the CIA,

00:51:49.200 --> 00:51:52.840
asked the Justice Department to secretly reopen the case.

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:56.000
And I had no idea that for the next three years

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:58.160
my emails were being intercepted,

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:00.320
my phone calls were being monitored,

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:03.360
and that there were teams of FBI agents following me,

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:07.240
not 24 hours a day, but sometimes,

00:52:07.240 --> 00:52:09.640
as many as six teams following me.

00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:13.080
And then finally in January of 2012,

00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:15.480
four years after I blew the whistle,

00:52:15.480 --> 00:52:19.040
I was charged with five felonies,

00:52:19.040 --> 00:52:22.680
including three counts of espionage.

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:26.600
So the espionage charges stemmed from the ABC interview

00:52:26.600 --> 00:52:29.920
and a follow-on interview I gave to the New York Times,

00:52:29.920 --> 00:52:31.600
to Scott Shane at the New York Times.

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:33.360
I hadn't committed espionage,

00:52:33.360 --> 00:52:35.080
and they knew I hadn't committed espionage.

00:52:35.080 --> 00:52:37.520
One of the things that we got in discovery

00:52:37.520 --> 00:52:41.960
was a series of memos between John Brennan and Eric Holder.

00:52:41.960 --> 00:52:44.040
In the first one, Brennan wrote,

00:52:44.040 --> 00:52:45.720
"'Charge him with espionage.'"

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:47.120
And Holder responded,

00:52:47.120 --> 00:52:49.720
"'My people don't think he committed espionage.'"

00:52:49.720 --> 00:52:51.360
And then Brennan wrote back and said,

00:52:51.400 --> 00:52:53.840
"'Charge him anyway and make him defend himself.'"

00:52:53.840 --> 00:52:56.320
And so that's what they did.

00:52:56.320 --> 00:52:58.880
In the end, I took a plea to a lesser count

00:52:58.880 --> 00:53:01.600
to violating the Intelligence Identities Protection Act

00:53:01.600 --> 00:53:03.120
of 1981.

00:53:03.120 --> 00:53:04.520
I'm only the second American

00:53:04.520 --> 00:53:07.800
who's ever been charged with that as a crime.

00:53:07.800 --> 00:53:09.440
And that was for the, I'm sorry to interrupt,

00:53:09.440 --> 00:53:11.760
that was for identifying,

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:14.560
you were speaking to, was it Matthew Cole, right?

00:53:14.560 --> 00:53:16.120
Yes, yes, correct.

00:53:16.120 --> 00:53:17.680
And sort of inadvertently he asked,

00:53:17.680 --> 00:53:20.480
he sort of prompts you to,

00:53:20.520 --> 00:53:21.400
just to give the background,

00:53:21.400 --> 00:53:24.800
you were a CIA officer for 14 years.

00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:27.440
And basically these reporters were after,

00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:31.640
sort of the story about the use of waterboarding

00:53:31.640 --> 00:53:34.440
and torture, especially with Abu Zubidah,

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:35.960
how do you pronounce that, Zubidah, right?

00:53:35.960 --> 00:53:36.800
Is that, yeah.

00:53:36.800 --> 00:53:39.320
Zubidah, right, Zubidah, right, Zubidah.

00:53:39.320 --> 00:53:42.080
And so they were basically,

00:53:42.080 --> 00:53:43.960
he was prompting you for a name

00:53:43.960 --> 00:53:46.800
and you sort of gave it, basically, right?

00:53:46.800 --> 00:53:47.640
Is that what happened?

00:53:47.640 --> 00:53:49.000
Yeah, what he said was,

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:50.880
he told me he was writing a book

00:53:50.880 --> 00:53:52.640
on the Abu Omar rendition,

00:53:52.640 --> 00:53:55.880
which is a rendition that's been covered in the media.

00:53:55.880 --> 00:53:57.720
It took place in Milan.

00:53:57.720 --> 00:54:00.800
We snatched this Imam and sent him to Egypt to be tortured

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:02.520
and it turned out he was innocent.

00:54:02.520 --> 00:54:04.120
So he asked me,

00:54:04.120 --> 00:54:07.480
do you know any of these 12 names?

00:54:07.480 --> 00:54:09.240
He asked me in an email and I said,

00:54:09.240 --> 00:54:11.200
I don't know any of these guys.

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:13.400
Kidnapping wasn't my thing at the CIA.

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:15.480
I didn't participate in kidnappings.

00:54:15.480 --> 00:54:18.980
And he sent me a second list of 12 names

00:54:18.980 --> 00:54:20.460
and I responded saying,

00:54:20.460 --> 00:54:23.900
you obviously know this issue far better than I do.

00:54:23.900 --> 00:54:26.300
I really don't know any of these people.

00:54:26.300 --> 00:54:27.140
And then he said,

00:54:27.140 --> 00:54:29.680
what about the guy that you mentioned on page,

00:54:29.680 --> 00:54:31.700
whatever, of your first book?

00:54:31.700 --> 00:54:33.460
I think his name is John.

00:54:33.460 --> 00:54:38.020
And I said, oh, you're talking about John Smith.

00:54:38.020 --> 00:54:39.380
I don't know whatever happened to him.

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:41.940
He's probably retired and living somewhere

00:54:41.940 --> 00:54:43.820
in Northern Virginia.

00:54:43.820 --> 00:54:46.260
I confirmed that name to him.

00:54:46.260 --> 00:54:47.660
And so he took that name,

00:54:47.660 --> 00:54:49.900
he gave it to the Guantanamo defense attorneys.

00:54:49.900 --> 00:54:54.220
They put it in a classified motion to the judge

00:54:54.220 --> 00:54:58.060
asking for permission to interview John Smith.

00:54:58.060 --> 00:55:00.300
The judge recognized the name as classified.

00:55:00.300 --> 00:55:02.280
He turned it over to the FBI.

00:55:02.280 --> 00:55:06.180
The FBI then traced the information back

00:55:06.180 --> 00:55:08.460
from the Guantanamo defense attorneys,

00:55:08.460 --> 00:55:10.940
eventually to Matthew Cole.

00:55:10.940 --> 00:55:13.940
And then I don't know if Matthew Cole

00:55:13.940 --> 00:55:15.740
ratted me out to the FBI

00:55:15.740 --> 00:55:19.020
or perhaps the FBI went into his emails,

00:55:19.020 --> 00:55:20.580
although he was a working journalist

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:22.140
and I find that hard to believe.

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:23.820
But either way, they traced it back to me

00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:26.220
and then I was arrested and charged for it.

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:27.220
Who is Matthew Cole?

00:55:27.220 --> 00:55:29.940
Is he known as a journalist or?

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:32.100
Yeah, he's with the intercept now.

00:55:32.100 --> 00:55:34.860
He doesn't write much, a couple of pieces a year.

00:55:34.860 --> 00:55:36.380
I understand that he married money

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:38.540
so he doesn't need the salary.

00:55:39.940 --> 00:55:43.740
But he was briefly with ABC, briefly independent.

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:45.700
He went to NBC for a little while

00:55:45.700 --> 00:55:47.060
and now he's with the intercept.

00:55:47.060 --> 00:55:48.860
The really amazing part of your story to me

00:55:48.860 --> 00:55:52.460
was the espionage charge was just predicated

00:55:52.460 --> 00:55:54.500
on the idea that you talked to a reporter,

00:55:54.500 --> 00:55:55.860
an American reporter, right?

00:55:55.860 --> 00:55:58.300
Or two American reporters basically, right?

00:55:58.300 --> 00:55:59.420
Correct, yes.

00:55:59.420 --> 00:56:01.940
You weren't the only person that this happened to, right?

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:04.900
There were a bunch of people,

00:56:04.900 --> 00:56:06.260
especially in the Obama years

00:56:06.260 --> 00:56:08.620
that they used this trick.

00:56:08.620 --> 00:56:11.460
And it of course has a chilling effect

00:56:11.500 --> 00:56:13.100
or it has to.

00:56:14.500 --> 00:56:17.300
And obviously you had to really worry

00:56:17.300 --> 00:56:18.700
about the gravity of that charge.

00:56:18.700 --> 00:56:20.620
I mean, how many years would that have carried

00:56:20.620 --> 00:56:21.740
if you had been given?

00:56:21.740 --> 00:56:26.740
45, I was, you know, on the day of my arraignment

00:56:27.060 --> 00:56:29.780
the feds offered me 45 years.

00:56:29.780 --> 00:56:32.700
They said, take a plea, you do 45 years

00:56:32.700 --> 00:56:35.420
and you might live to meet your grandchildren.

00:56:35.420 --> 00:56:38.220
And I remember like just being kind of in a fog.

00:56:38.220 --> 00:56:40.500
I was in a state of shock.

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:44.340
And I remember saying, I'm not doing 45 minutes.

00:56:44.340 --> 00:56:46.340
I didn't do anything wrong.

00:56:46.340 --> 00:56:49.020
And then a couple of months passed

00:56:49.020 --> 00:56:51.820
and they came back with an offer of 10 years.

00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:55.420
They said, take a plea to espionage and you do 10 years.

00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:57.980
Get out in eight and a half.

00:56:58.860 --> 00:57:00.580
And we rejected it.

00:57:00.580 --> 00:57:01.620
That was on a Monday.

00:57:01.620 --> 00:57:05.980
They came back on Wednesday with eight years

00:57:05.980 --> 00:57:07.580
and on Friday with five years.

00:57:07.620 --> 00:57:11.140
And my lead attorney was a guy named Plato Kacheris,

00:57:11.140 --> 00:57:14.820
legendary legal figure in Washington.

00:57:14.820 --> 00:57:17.540
He said to me, you know, I've been practicing law

00:57:17.540 --> 00:57:20.420
in Washington for 53 years

00:57:20.420 --> 00:57:23.020
and I've never seen them come down in time.

00:57:23.020 --> 00:57:26.220
If you reject a plea, they always go up in time, right?

00:57:26.220 --> 00:57:27.060
They offer you 10.

00:57:27.060 --> 00:57:30.220
You say, no, they go to 12, then they go to 15.

00:57:30.220 --> 00:57:31.980
I said, why are they coming down?

00:57:31.980 --> 00:57:33.780
And he said, because they have a shit case

00:57:33.780 --> 00:57:37.060
and they know it's shit and we're going to trial.

00:57:37.060 --> 00:57:40.980
And so that conversation was in March of 2012.

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:45.980
We went all the way up to trial in October of 2012

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:49.300
before we finally negotiated a plea.

00:57:49.300 --> 00:57:51.140
And what was the plea?

00:57:51.140 --> 00:57:52.640
The plea was to plead guilty

00:57:52.640 --> 00:57:55.820
to violating the Intelligence Identities Protection Act.

00:57:55.820 --> 00:57:59.100
And I got 30 months in prison.

00:57:59.100 --> 00:58:01.180
I did 23 months.

00:58:01.180 --> 00:58:03.740
And did you have any idea when you spoke out

00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:05.660
about this torture program basically

00:58:05.660 --> 00:58:08.700
that you would be facing potential jail time?

00:58:08.700 --> 00:58:09.540
No.

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:11.620
And the reason why I didn't think so

00:58:11.620 --> 00:58:14.260
was because there are laws in this country

00:58:14.260 --> 00:58:17.300
that make it illegal to classify a crime.

00:58:17.300 --> 00:58:19.800
So if something is a criminal act

00:58:19.800 --> 00:58:21.820
or if it is classified solely

00:58:21.820 --> 00:58:23.700
because it's embarrassing to the CIA

00:58:23.700 --> 00:58:25.980
or the intelligence community,

00:58:25.980 --> 00:58:28.000
it's improperly classified,

00:58:28.000 --> 00:58:30.740
which in and of itself is a felony.

00:58:30.740 --> 00:58:33.980
And so I believe then and I believe now

00:58:34.020 --> 00:58:37.180
that the torture program was a crime.

00:58:37.180 --> 00:58:39.980
And so exposing it was not a criminal act.

00:58:39.980 --> 00:58:42.180
In my view, it was a patriotic act.

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:43.740
You also in the book you talk about

00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:46.420
how you left the CIA in 2004.

00:58:46.420 --> 00:58:47.980
You just hadn't been following the news.

00:58:47.980 --> 00:58:50.980
You assumed that that was sort of widely known, right?

00:58:50.980 --> 00:58:51.820
Yeah.

00:58:51.820 --> 00:58:54.980
And they were coming to you, right?

00:58:54.980 --> 00:58:55.820
Yeah.

00:58:55.820 --> 00:58:56.780
People came to me all the time.

00:58:56.780 --> 00:58:58.020
Journalists came to me all the time

00:58:58.020 --> 00:59:00.460
for background on the torture program.

00:59:00.460 --> 00:59:03.300
And I really had tuned it out.

00:59:03.300 --> 00:59:08.140
I stopped focusing on it and went on with my life.

00:59:08.140 --> 00:59:10.980
And I just didn't think that I was saying anything

00:59:10.980 --> 00:59:12.540
that was particularly sensitive.

00:59:12.540 --> 00:59:15.220
You said that Brennan was a long time nemesis of yours.

00:59:15.220 --> 00:59:16.060
What was that about?

00:59:16.060 --> 00:59:17.900
Like what are the roots of that?

00:59:17.900 --> 00:59:20.180
You know, John was one of these alpha dogs.

00:59:20.180 --> 00:59:22.660
He looks so nice, his face, just kidding.

00:59:22.660 --> 00:59:24.660
Yeah, right, his face.

00:59:24.660 --> 00:59:26.260
Face only a mother could love.

00:59:26.260 --> 00:59:27.420
Probably not even that.

00:59:27.420 --> 00:59:29.580
You took the words right out of my mouth.

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:32.460
So when I first joined the CIA,

00:59:32.460 --> 00:59:34.820
John was a GS-15,

00:59:34.820 --> 00:59:36.060
he was the deputy director

00:59:36.060 --> 00:59:39.580
of the Arab-Israeli Analysis Group, right?

00:59:39.580 --> 00:59:42.220
No big deal, not terribly busy.

00:59:42.220 --> 00:59:45.180
And there's kind of a famous story inside the CIA.

00:59:45.180 --> 00:59:49.020
He went to his boss, a woman named Martha Kessler.

00:59:49.020 --> 00:59:50.540
Martha was the director

00:59:50.540 --> 00:59:54.420
of that Arab-Israeli Intelligence or Analysis Group.

00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:57.540
Martha was legendary because she was a published author,

00:59:57.540 --> 01:00:00.220
which is unusual for a CIA officer.

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:05.000
sitting CIA officer. She wrote this book called Syria fragile mosaic of power

01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:09.760
that we all had to read because it was the definitive work on Syria at the time.

01:00:09.760 --> 01:00:14.800
So she didn't like John and John was imposed on her and John went to her

01:00:14.800 --> 01:00:19.920
and said Martha I think I'm ready to be promoted to the senior intelligence

01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:25.120
service and I want your blessing and she said not only will you never be

01:00:25.120 --> 01:00:29.640
promoted to the senior intelligence service. I don't even want you working

01:00:29.640 --> 01:00:34.280
for me anymore and she fired him. Wow. Now when you're fired at the CIA you're

01:00:34.280 --> 01:00:40.560
not actually literally fired. You have six weeks to walk the halls and find another

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:44.920
job and if you find another job you're you're good. If you don't find another

01:00:44.920 --> 01:00:49.240
job then they escort you to your car and tell you never to come back. Well this

01:00:49.240 --> 01:00:55.440
happened the week before Christmas 1996 and there are no jobs open the week

01:00:55.440 --> 01:01:00.960
before Christmas the normal turnover is in the summer. So John started walking

01:01:00.960 --> 01:01:05.680
the halls and finally he went to the PDB staff the president's daily brief and

01:01:05.680 --> 01:01:09.840
said that he really really needed a job and so they had an opening and they

01:01:09.840 --> 01:01:15.360
gave him a job doing the PDB briefing every day for the lowest ranking

01:01:15.360 --> 01:01:19.920
official entitled to a PDB briefing and that was the national security

01:01:19.920 --> 01:01:24.640
council's director of intelligence programs who at the time happened to

01:01:24.640 --> 01:01:32.400
be George Tennant. They immediately hit it off. They're both like nasty cigar

01:01:32.400 --> 01:01:41.200
smoking, filthy mouth, cheating on their wives. They're just awful both of them. So

01:01:41.200 --> 01:01:46.160
when you know a couple of months later George gets promoted and he becomes the

01:01:46.160 --> 01:01:52.680
deputy director of the CIA. He brings John back to the CIA with him and he

01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:57.840
makes John the deputy director of the office of Near Eastern and South Asian

01:01:57.840 --> 01:02:03.720
analysis Martha Kessler's boss. Oh wow. Poor Martha. The first thing he does is he

01:02:03.720 --> 01:02:10.120
calls her in and he says now you're fired. And so Martha packed up her stuff

01:02:10.120 --> 01:02:14.760
and walked out none of us ever saw her again. George took good care of John

01:02:14.760 --> 01:02:20.040
because they were friends. John had never served overseas before but George

01:02:20.080 --> 01:02:24.480
made him the station chief in Riyadh with direct access to the king, for

01:02:24.480 --> 01:02:29.000
example. And then when he came back from Riyadh he made him the executive

01:02:29.000 --> 01:02:34.240
director of the CIA, the third ranking official in the CIA. John was in over

01:02:34.240 --> 01:02:42.360
his head intellectually but he was a terrific political knife fighter. And

01:02:42.360 --> 01:02:50.840
you know that was just never me. I was kind of the substantive nerdy guy. I

01:02:50.840 --> 01:02:55.960
ended up transitioning from analysis to operations and found that I had a

01:02:55.960 --> 01:03:01.200
knack recruiting people. Your job is to recruit spies to steal secrets for

01:03:01.200 --> 01:03:05.760
you. I had a knack for it. I have a facility for foreign languages. So

01:03:05.760 --> 01:03:10.200
John and I were on two completely different tracks at the CIA. He was

01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:17.080
all politics. I see. So we'll get back to John and Nibi but there's a couple of

01:03:17.080 --> 01:03:18.720
other things that are in the news that I really want to ask you about it.

01:03:18.720 --> 01:03:25.560
Actually, so recently there was a furor about Hillary Clinton and talking

01:03:25.560 --> 01:03:29.160
essentially about Tulsi Gabbard. There's controversy about the word

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:36.360
asset. And you were in the CIA. What does the word asset mean? The word

01:03:36.400 --> 01:03:41.480
asset has a very specific meaning in the CIA and the broader intelligence

01:03:41.480 --> 01:03:47.440
community. Asset means that you are being paid to provide either classified

01:03:47.440 --> 01:03:53.600
information or access to someone who has classified information. In nine

01:03:54.840 --> 01:03:58.760
times out of 10, 90% of the time, you know that you were being paid by

01:03:58.760 --> 01:04:02.680
the CIA or by an intelligence service and you were providing that

01:04:02.680 --> 01:04:08.400
service for cash. So to and Hillary Clinton knows this. Right. Hillary

01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:12.240
Clinton was Secretary of State. She had a PDB briefing every day. She knew

01:04:12.240 --> 01:04:17.920
all about the terminology. So man, when I when I heard that word come out

01:04:17.920 --> 01:04:22.120
of her mouth, I thought to myself, blood's going to be spilled over this.

01:04:22.120 --> 01:04:27.680
You can't you can't just accuse someone of being a paid agent of a

01:04:27.680 --> 01:04:31.720
Russian intelligence service and not expect for there to be fallout. But

01:04:31.760 --> 01:04:35.120
they kind of fell back. Oh, my gosh. Oh, well, we're not saying the

01:04:35.120 --> 01:04:39.080
person's a spy. Like, you know, it's unwitting. Yeah, they actually

01:04:39.080 --> 01:04:43.240
mean they almost mean useful idiot. Like, yeah, yeah, which is the name of

01:04:43.240 --> 01:04:48.040
our show. But but they're pretending that asset means unintentional. Yes.

01:04:48.240 --> 01:04:52.600
Yes. That was that was some fancy footwork to cover up a major

01:04:52.640 --> 01:04:55.920
boner that she had made. Right. I agree with you. What she meant

01:04:55.920 --> 01:04:59.160
was useful idiot, something that I've been accused of being as

01:04:59.160 --> 01:05:03.520
well. But you know, I think that that that just doesn't fly. Yeah.

01:05:03.600 --> 01:05:06.240
Another thing that I think was the story where you have some

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:10.600
expertise and are able to talk about sort of the this notion of

01:05:10.600 --> 01:05:12.880
what is a whistleblower? What isn't a whistleblower? You and I have

01:05:12.880 --> 01:05:20.360
spoken about this before. When this Ukraine story broke, the there

01:05:20.360 --> 01:05:25.200
were suddenly all this stuff in the news about Oh, this we have to

01:05:25.200 --> 01:05:28.920
protect whistleblowers. Let's, you know, they occupy such an

01:05:28.920 --> 01:05:33.080
important role in society that I just struck my ear so so strangely

01:05:33.080 --> 01:05:36.320
since we that suddenly like a newfound revelation on the part of

01:05:36.320 --> 01:05:38.880
the press. What did you think when you started seeing the word

01:05:38.880 --> 01:05:41.840
whistleblower bandied about and this person isn't necessarily

01:05:41.840 --> 01:05:45.640
not a whistleblower, but it just right. It was odd to me. I

01:05:45.640 --> 01:05:47.800
wondered what you thought. Well, first, I want to say how

01:05:47.800 --> 01:05:51.360
much I enjoyed that article that you wrote in Rolling Stone. I

01:05:51.360 --> 01:05:54.320
got more mileage out of that article than I think I have

01:05:54.320 --> 01:05:57.320
out of any other article the last five years that has

01:05:57.360 --> 01:06:01.440
mentioned me. In fact, it was your article, Matt, that that

01:06:01.440 --> 01:06:05.240
got me on to the Tucker Carlson show that night. Wow. And I

01:06:05.240 --> 01:06:08.760
repeated to Tucker what I had told you. And then sure enough,

01:06:08.760 --> 01:06:13.480
an hour later, Donald Trump tweeted it. I was shocked.

01:06:13.520 --> 01:06:16.600
Right. I've never been tweeted by Donald Trump before.

01:06:16.720 --> 01:06:19.080
But yeah, I was a little shocked, too. Yeah, it was

01:06:19.080 --> 01:06:22.840
shocked. Yeah, I'll repeat for for your viewers what you

01:06:22.840 --> 01:06:25.880
and I talked about that day. There's a legal definition of

01:06:25.920 --> 01:06:29.320
whistleblowing. It's bringing to light any evidence of waste,

01:06:29.320 --> 01:06:32.840
fraud, abuse, illegality or threats to the public health or

01:06:32.840 --> 01:06:36.760
public safety. Motivation is irrelevant, right, to be

01:06:36.760 --> 01:06:41.280
covered by the law. So with that said, my initial knee-jerk

01:06:41.280 --> 01:06:44.480
reaction was, Oh, there's a CIA whistleblower and he's

01:06:44.480 --> 01:06:47.320
blowing the whistle on what he believes to be waste,

01:06:47.320 --> 01:06:50.800
fraud, abuse or illegality at the White House. But then I

01:06:50.800 --> 01:06:56.680
read the complaint and the complaint just didn't add up. The

01:06:56.680 --> 01:07:00.800
law says that if a whistleblower comes across this

01:07:00.800 --> 01:07:03.720
information, he has to report it to his immediate supervisor

01:07:03.720 --> 01:07:05.840
and then he and the supervisor go to the inspector

01:07:05.840 --> 01:07:09.240
general. The inspector general does an investigation and

01:07:09.240 --> 01:07:11.840
then goes to the oversight committee. We know that

01:07:11.840 --> 01:07:14.960
that's not what happened in this case. What happened

01:07:14.960 --> 01:07:18.280
was he went to a supervisor, the supervisor went to the

01:07:18.280 --> 01:07:21.160
IG. That's all fine and good. But then the IG went to the

01:07:21.160 --> 01:07:23.800
general council. The general council got the lawyers

01:07:23.800 --> 01:07:26.960
together. They went to the CIA leadership, then back to the

01:07:26.960 --> 01:07:30.600
IG, and then the IG went to the DNI IG, the director of

01:07:30.600 --> 01:07:32.600
national intelligence, and then to the justice

01:07:32.600 --> 01:07:35.440
department. So the system just did not work the way it

01:07:35.440 --> 01:07:37.560
was supposed to work. And then when you read this

01:07:37.560 --> 01:07:41.520
report, this is not written in the CIA's writing

01:07:41.520 --> 01:07:44.800
style. We know that the that the so-called whistleblower

01:07:44.800 --> 01:07:47.200
is an analyst. I was an analyst the first seven

01:07:47.240 --> 01:07:50.560
years of my career. When you're hired as an analyst, you

01:07:50.560 --> 01:07:54.160
go to the farm, the CIA's training facility down

01:07:54.160 --> 01:07:58.440
south. And for six months, they beat into your head, the

01:07:58.440 --> 01:08:01.440
CIA's writing style, so that when you're writing for the

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:04.600
PDB or you're writing a paper for the secretary of

01:08:04.600 --> 01:08:08.080
whatever, no matter who's writing it, it looks exactly

01:08:08.080 --> 01:08:11.520
the same, right? The product has to be

01:08:11.520 --> 01:08:15.840
consistent. This was not written in the CIA's

01:08:15.840 --> 01:08:18.280
writing style. This was written in a legal writing

01:08:18.280 --> 01:08:22.000
style, which tells me that whatever information the

01:08:22.000 --> 01:08:25.400
whistleblower had or thought he had, it was morphed

01:08:25.400 --> 01:08:28.120
into something completely different by a team of

01:08:28.120 --> 01:08:31.880
attorneys and by the CIA leadership. And so I don't

01:08:31.880 --> 01:08:34.320
think this is a whistleblower. I think maybe he

01:08:34.320 --> 01:08:36.520
started out as a whistleblower, but I think the

01:08:36.520 --> 01:08:40.160
whole process was hijacked by the CIA's leadership and

01:08:40.160 --> 01:08:41.280
the CIA's attorneys.

01:08:41.400 --> 01:08:49.000
Right, right. And do you think that there was more

01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:51.360
than one person? I mean, it was very odd reading the

01:08:51.360 --> 01:08:54.320
complaint. It even specifically references the

01:08:54.320 --> 01:08:58.880
idea that the information came in conversations with

01:08:58.880 --> 01:09:02.520
multiple colleagues. It seems like a normal

01:09:02.520 --> 01:09:06.160
whistleblower wouldn't be able to share what was

01:09:06.160 --> 01:09:10.840
going on if you're at work. That's part of why

01:09:10.880 --> 01:09:13.000
you blow the whistle, isn't it? I mean, I thought that

01:09:13.000 --> 01:09:16.720
was an odd detail. That's right. And presidential

01:09:16.720 --> 01:09:19.840
conversations like this are compartmentalized. So

01:09:19.840 --> 01:09:23.520
even if the whistleblower had access to the

01:09:23.520 --> 01:09:26.440
transcript or to the actual conversation, he would

01:09:26.440 --> 01:09:29.400
be forbidden from sharing it with his colleagues

01:09:29.800 --> 01:09:31.840
because the colleagues aren't cleared for it. They

01:09:31.840 --> 01:09:34.440
don't have a need to know. And so that bothered

01:09:34.440 --> 01:09:37.120
me too. We know from the New York Times and we

01:09:37.120 --> 01:09:39.440
know from Mark Zade, and we can talk about Mark

01:09:39.440 --> 01:09:42.720
Zade in a minute, who's the attorney representing

01:09:42.720 --> 01:09:44.800
the whistleblower, that there are actually, there

01:09:44.800 --> 01:09:46.840
were two whistleblowers. Mark Zade apparently

01:09:46.840 --> 01:09:49.440
represents both of them. But there may have been

01:09:49.440 --> 01:09:51.320
more, and we know that there were these

01:09:51.320 --> 01:09:54.680
conversations that were taking place in the

01:09:54.680 --> 01:09:58.640
hallways with colleagues. So the whole situation

01:09:58.640 --> 01:09:59.440
is so muddy.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:03.800
that I think that we really don't know what happened.

01:10:03.800 --> 01:10:11.400
But what we've been presented with this report from the CIA,

01:10:11.400 --> 01:10:12.640
it just doesn't stand up.

01:10:12.640 --> 01:10:14.880
You're a lifelong Democrat.

01:10:14.880 --> 01:10:19.600
I heard your really great interview with Paul Jay

01:10:19.600 --> 01:10:21.360
and you talk about how you like joined it.

01:10:21.360 --> 01:10:21.880
Wow, that was a long time ago.

01:10:21.880 --> 01:10:23.160
Yeah, it was great.

01:10:23.160 --> 01:10:24.720
You talk about how you started out

01:10:24.720 --> 01:10:28.200
your political career at like eight stuffing envelopes

01:10:28.200 --> 01:10:30.480
from Regovern.

01:10:30.480 --> 01:10:33.800
And like that was me with the caucus, too.

01:10:33.800 --> 01:10:34.840
So we have that in common.

01:10:34.840 --> 01:10:37.080
But it's interesting how this whole,

01:10:37.080 --> 01:10:39.400
there's been this political realignment.

01:10:39.400 --> 01:10:44.320
So you're a lifelong Democrat and right now Trump

01:10:44.320 --> 01:10:47.800
may be your only hope for pardoning.

01:10:47.800 --> 01:10:50.640
You went on, you had to go on Tucker Carlson, right?

01:10:50.640 --> 01:10:52.840
And we talked about this a lot, how like what happens

01:10:52.840 --> 01:10:55.760
is the kind of there's this Democratic mainstream

01:10:55.760 --> 01:10:56.760
liberal media.

01:10:56.760 --> 01:10:59.040
And even saying that, I sound like a right winger.

01:10:59.040 --> 01:11:02.760
But like because there's no audience anywhere else,

01:11:02.760 --> 01:11:05.280
if you want to express what you want to say,

01:11:05.280 --> 01:11:09.080
you are forced onto someone show like Tucker Carlson.

01:11:09.080 --> 01:11:11.320
And do you get called a Trumpian?

01:11:11.320 --> 01:11:13.080
Like can you talk about this experience

01:11:13.080 --> 01:11:15.520
and how you deal with it?

01:11:15.520 --> 01:11:19.440
It's so bizarre to me that anybody

01:11:19.440 --> 01:11:22.200
would think that I was a Trump supporter.

01:11:22.200 --> 01:11:25.440
I mean, it's just, it's crazy.

01:11:25.440 --> 01:11:29.400
Tucker Carlson is 100% right on whistleblower issues.

01:11:29.400 --> 01:11:32.160
He's always been right on whistleblower issues.

01:11:32.160 --> 01:11:34.040
He was an early supporter of mine,

01:11:34.040 --> 01:11:36.320
and I'm deeply indebted for that support.

01:11:36.320 --> 01:11:41.360
I think he's nuts on immigration and on some other issues.

01:11:41.360 --> 01:11:45.440
But by God on whistleblowers, he is entirely correct.

01:11:45.440 --> 01:11:48.080
As crazy as things have gotten, I

01:11:48.080 --> 01:11:51.800
was invited to Pat Buchanan's Christmas party.

01:11:51.800 --> 01:11:53.880
You can imagine such a thing.

01:11:53.880 --> 01:11:56.560
But you're right, I was a third-generation Democrat.

01:11:56.560 --> 01:11:59.760
My grandfather had a framed picture of Franklin Roosevelt

01:11:59.760 --> 01:12:03.840
on the top of the TV that was there as long as he was alive.

01:12:03.840 --> 01:12:07.120
And I left the Democratic Party in 2012

01:12:07.120 --> 01:12:10.320
only because of what the Obama administration did to me.

01:12:10.320 --> 01:12:12.960
I've come to believe that so many Democrats

01:12:12.960 --> 01:12:16.800
are so conservative in that war-loving neo-liberal way.

01:12:16.800 --> 01:12:17.600
Exactly.

01:12:17.600 --> 01:12:19.440
I just, I can't anymore.

01:12:19.440 --> 01:12:21.160
It's almost neo-carnish in some ways.

01:12:21.160 --> 01:12:22.760
It's not even just neoliberal, right?

01:12:22.760 --> 01:12:25.560
Because they actually support the war agenda, yeah.

01:12:25.560 --> 01:12:27.520
Yeah, I don't find much of a difference

01:12:27.520 --> 01:12:29.840
between the neo-cons and the neoliberals.

01:12:29.840 --> 01:12:32.160
At the end of the day, they support the same thing.

01:12:32.160 --> 01:12:35.120
Wars for oil, wherever we might find them.

01:12:35.120 --> 01:12:39.480
And so, yeah, I left the Democratic Party.

01:12:39.480 --> 01:12:43.200
I would love to go on CNN and talk about these issues,

01:12:43.200 --> 01:12:44.880
but I'm not invited.

01:12:44.880 --> 01:12:46.760
I'd love to go on MSNBC.

01:12:46.760 --> 01:12:49.880
And on rare occasions, I'm invited to MSNBC.

01:12:49.880 --> 01:12:52.160
But I have problems with MSNBC.

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:55.840
I did the Ari Melbers show a couple of weeks ago.

01:12:55.840 --> 01:12:57.720
And he called and said, hey, we're

01:12:57.720 --> 01:12:59.040
going to have Dan Ellsberg.

01:12:59.040 --> 01:13:00.480
And Dan's a good friend of mine.

01:13:00.480 --> 01:13:02.720
I said, yeah, I'd love to be on with Dan.

01:13:02.720 --> 01:13:03.880
And so I went on.

01:13:03.880 --> 01:13:05.960
Dan had already taped his segment.

01:13:05.960 --> 01:13:09.320
So I went in, wearing my suit, freshly shaven,

01:13:09.320 --> 01:13:10.280
which I'm not now.

01:13:10.280 --> 01:13:11.080
I apologize.

01:13:11.080 --> 01:13:11.680
That's all right.

01:13:11.680 --> 01:13:14.080
He liked the authentic look.

01:13:14.080 --> 01:13:15.200
And I go on.

01:13:15.200 --> 01:13:18.400
And he introduces me as former CIA leaker

01:13:18.400 --> 01:13:22.000
and convicted felon John Kiriaki.

01:13:22.000 --> 01:13:24.240
And I said to him, can I swear on this show?

01:13:24.240 --> 01:13:24.960
Yeah.

01:13:24.960 --> 01:13:26.080
Yeah, of course.

01:13:26.080 --> 01:13:29.520
I said, motherfucker, is that what this is?

01:13:29.520 --> 01:13:30.680
Is this a setup?

01:13:30.680 --> 01:13:34.360
And I went to take off my microphone.

01:13:34.360 --> 01:13:36.840
And he says, no, no, wait, wait, wait, sorry.

01:13:36.840 --> 01:13:37.920
What happened?

01:13:37.920 --> 01:13:39.280
What's wrong?

01:13:39.280 --> 01:13:41.680
I said, you're going to introduce me

01:13:41.680 --> 01:13:42.840
as a convicted felon.

01:13:42.840 --> 01:13:44.440
That's what this is about.

01:13:44.440 --> 01:13:46.960
I'm happy to talk about what I did and the fact

01:13:46.960 --> 01:13:50.360
that I have zero remorse over what I did.

01:13:50.360 --> 01:13:52.880
But if you're going to try to undercut my credibility

01:13:52.880 --> 01:13:57.920
from your opening statement, I'm not playing games with you.

01:13:57.920 --> 01:14:00.520
And so we redid the whole thing.

01:14:00.520 --> 01:14:03.040
And leak or not whistleblower also.

01:14:03.040 --> 01:14:04.400
And leak or not whistleblower.

01:14:04.400 --> 01:14:07.560
And I'll tell you what, it took my lawyers a year

01:14:07.560 --> 01:14:11.680
to get CNN and MSNBC to stop calling me CIA leaker

01:14:11.680 --> 01:14:14.680
John Kiriaki and to start calling me CIA whistleblower

01:14:14.680 --> 01:14:15.520
John Kiriaki.

01:14:15.520 --> 01:14:17.200
CNN did it first.

01:14:17.200 --> 01:14:19.920
MSNBC didn't do it until after I was in prison.

01:14:19.960 --> 01:14:22.600
And then even now, well, I've turned down

01:14:22.600 --> 01:14:25.360
three requests to do the Rachel Maddow show

01:14:25.360 --> 01:14:29.280
because she insists on referring to me as John Kiriaki

01:14:29.280 --> 01:14:31.840
who styles himself a whistleblower.

01:14:31.840 --> 01:14:32.480
Oh my god.

01:14:32.480 --> 01:14:33.080
Styles.

01:14:33.080 --> 01:14:36.480
Yeah, so I don't need to do these interviews.

01:14:36.480 --> 01:14:39.160
I don't gain anything from these interviews.

01:14:39.160 --> 01:14:43.840
So why would I subject myself to harassment like this?

01:14:43.840 --> 01:14:45.360
Just so they can get their digs in.

01:14:45.360 --> 01:14:46.480
It's just not worth it to me.

01:14:46.480 --> 01:14:47.640
I'm not doing it anymore.

01:14:47.680 --> 01:14:50.520
And I'll tell you the truth, I just can't watch MSNBC anymore.

01:14:50.520 --> 01:14:51.320
No, they're going to wait.

01:14:51.320 --> 01:14:53.240
I mean, for comedic value, I do.

01:14:53.240 --> 01:14:55.480
Or if I want to teach them.

01:14:55.480 --> 01:14:57.520
And they weren't terribly, it turned out in the end,

01:14:57.520 --> 01:14:58.920
terribly interested in your point of view

01:14:58.920 --> 01:15:00.680
on the whistleblower story anyway, right?

01:15:00.680 --> 01:15:03.760
Yeah, they thought you were going to be like, yeah, rah-rah.

01:15:03.760 --> 01:15:06.200
But you weren't even saying negative things.

01:15:06.200 --> 01:15:09.880
You were just saying, we just have to kind of wait and see.

01:15:09.880 --> 01:15:12.160
That was the funny thing about the whole interview.

01:15:12.160 --> 01:15:15.160
The substance of the interview was him asking me nine

01:15:15.200 --> 01:15:20.120
ways from Sunday to endorse what the CIA whistleblower was

01:15:20.120 --> 01:15:20.620
saying.

01:15:20.620 --> 01:15:23.760
And I kept saying, but I don't think this is a whistleblower.

01:15:23.760 --> 01:15:26.760
You know, there really is such a thing as a deep state.

01:15:26.760 --> 01:15:28.720
You don't have to call it the deep state.

01:15:28.720 --> 01:15:30.640
You don't have to be conspiratorial about it.

01:15:30.640 --> 01:15:33.960
You can just call it like I call it the federal bureaucracy.

01:15:33.960 --> 01:15:38.120
And at the CIA, we have this thing, this leadership level

01:15:38.120 --> 01:15:41.480
called the senior intelligence service, SIS.

01:15:41.480 --> 01:15:44.160
And people who have been or who are SIS

01:15:44.160 --> 01:15:49.160
have been at the CIA for 25 years and 30 years and 35 years.

01:15:49.160 --> 01:15:50.920
And they know that they can outweigh presidents.

01:15:50.920 --> 01:15:52.440
They can outweigh this president.

01:15:52.440 --> 01:15:55.480
Presidents come and go, and they don't.

01:15:55.480 --> 01:15:56.760
They're there forever.

01:15:56.760 --> 01:15:59.680
So if you don't like Jimmy Carter, which they didn't,

01:15:59.680 --> 01:16:03.960
or Bill Clinton, or Donald Trump, you just ignore the doubt.

01:16:03.960 --> 01:16:05.880
And you wait for him to go away after four years

01:16:05.880 --> 01:16:07.480
or eight years.

01:16:07.480 --> 01:16:10.520
That's what the deep state is to me.

01:16:10.560 --> 01:16:11.960
I like that federal bureaucracy.

01:16:11.960 --> 01:16:12.640
Yeah, I know that.

01:16:12.640 --> 01:16:13.400
That'll be easier.

01:16:13.400 --> 01:16:15.920
Yeah, no, it's another thing they use, the conspiracy theorist

01:16:15.920 --> 01:16:18.920
or the Trumpian to discredit people.

01:16:18.920 --> 01:16:21.360
Yeah, and they even got to lean into the word,

01:16:21.360 --> 01:16:24.440
to the term deep state in order to discredit it.

01:16:24.440 --> 01:16:28.400
Right, they bring it up as many as many times as they can.

01:16:28.400 --> 01:16:29.440
Just to, yeah.

01:16:29.440 --> 01:16:30.240
Absolutely right.

01:16:30.240 --> 01:16:30.880
Yeah.

01:16:30.880 --> 01:16:34.800
So you wrote a book that I just finished reading this

01:16:34.800 --> 01:16:37.200
weekend, Doing Time Like a Spy.

01:16:37.200 --> 01:16:39.240
And we were talking about this off air.

01:16:39.240 --> 01:16:40.880
I don't know why.

01:16:40.880 --> 01:16:43.440
Well, apparently, there's some news in this front.

01:16:43.440 --> 01:16:44.880
But this should be a movie.

01:16:44.880 --> 01:16:49.400
So the premise of this book, it's all true,

01:16:49.400 --> 01:16:53.960
you're set to do your 30 months for the crime

01:16:53.960 --> 01:16:55.080
that you talked about.

01:16:55.080 --> 01:16:57.840
And you think you're going to go to minimum security work

01:16:57.840 --> 01:17:00.400
camp in Loretto, Pennsylvania.

01:17:00.400 --> 01:17:02.960
And you show up, and I think probably most people

01:17:02.960 --> 01:17:04.960
don't realize this, that even if you're

01:17:04.960 --> 01:17:07.160
recommended to go to minimum security prison,

01:17:07.280 --> 01:17:09.080
prison doesn't have to do that, right?

01:17:09.080 --> 01:17:11.040
Yeah, they can just sort of decide.

01:17:11.040 --> 01:17:13.760
So you show up and you end up in real prison.

01:17:13.760 --> 01:17:15.800
And basically, the book is about how

01:17:15.800 --> 01:17:21.160
you used your CIA skills to negotiate two years of hard time.

01:17:21.160 --> 01:17:25.160
And so talk first about that.

01:17:25.160 --> 01:17:26.680
What was that first realization

01:17:26.680 --> 01:17:30.640
like when you realize, oh my god, I'm going to have to do

01:17:30.640 --> 01:17:34.000
something, be in a real prison for a couple of years

01:17:34.000 --> 01:17:37.120
or more, and how are you going to navigate that?

01:17:37.760 --> 01:17:40.760
When you first go to prison, it's the oddest experience.

01:17:40.760 --> 01:17:41.920
You're in a state of shock.

01:17:41.920 --> 01:17:44.920
You can't believe that this is happening to you.

01:17:44.920 --> 01:17:49.720
And what you do is you literally just drive to the prison

01:17:49.720 --> 01:17:52.520
or have somebody drive to the prison.

01:17:52.520 --> 01:17:56.000
And you knock on the door and you say, hi,

01:17:56.000 --> 01:17:58.800
I'm John Curriaco, I'm here to turn myself in.

01:17:58.800 --> 01:18:01.400
And they say, oh, okay, come on in.

01:18:01.400 --> 01:18:03.640
They put you through the metal detector

01:18:03.640 --> 01:18:05.520
and then they process you.

01:18:05.520 --> 01:18:08.760
It's the most bizarre experience.

01:18:08.760 --> 01:18:11.960
So as you noted, the judge recommended

01:18:11.960 --> 01:18:14.080
that I go to the minimum security work camp.

01:18:14.080 --> 01:18:16.600
Camps are generally attached to a higher level prison

01:18:16.600 --> 01:18:19.240
across the street because in the event of a riot,

01:18:19.240 --> 01:18:21.000
it's the campers that do the cooking,

01:18:21.000 --> 01:18:24.400
do the laundry, mop the floors, make the prison run.

01:18:25.520 --> 01:18:28.600
And there are no bars on the windows, no fences,

01:18:28.600 --> 01:18:29.680
no locks on the doors.

01:18:29.680 --> 01:18:32.000
You're on your honor not to abscond.

01:18:32.000 --> 01:18:33.760
You can come and go as you please.

01:18:33.800 --> 01:18:35.540
And most people work in town,

01:18:35.540 --> 01:18:38.600
sweepin' floors at the local university or whatever.

01:18:38.600 --> 01:18:41.160
And so I got there and I turned myself in

01:18:41.160 --> 01:18:44.040
and the cop takes me outside

01:18:44.040 --> 01:18:46.880
and starts walking me around to the back

01:18:46.880 --> 01:18:48.040
of the actual prison.

01:18:48.040 --> 01:18:51.480
I said, no, no, I'm supposed to be at the camp

01:18:51.480 --> 01:18:53.080
across the street.

01:18:53.080 --> 01:18:55.280
And he kind of tortled.

01:18:55.280 --> 01:18:57.800
And he said, huh, not according to my paperwork,

01:18:57.800 --> 01:18:59.640
you're not.

01:18:59.640 --> 01:19:02.320
And I told myself, take it easy,

01:19:02.320 --> 01:19:05.360
there's nothing you can do if you raise a stink.

01:19:05.360 --> 01:19:07.160
They're gonna put you in solitary.

01:19:07.160 --> 01:19:08.280
So don't say anything.

01:19:08.280 --> 01:19:10.320
So I didn't, I didn't say anything.

01:19:10.320 --> 01:19:15.280
And it took me five days to get access to a phone.

01:19:15.280 --> 01:19:17.520
My first call was to my lawyer.

01:19:17.520 --> 01:19:21.440
And I said, hey, they put me in the actual prison

01:19:21.440 --> 01:19:24.120
with the drug dealers and the mafia dons

01:19:24.120 --> 01:19:25.640
and the pedophiles.

01:19:25.640 --> 01:19:26.480
What do I do?

01:19:26.480 --> 01:19:27.320
The chomos.

01:19:27.320 --> 01:19:28.760
The chomos.

01:19:28.760 --> 01:19:29.920
Which was a whole other experience.

01:19:29.920 --> 01:19:32.200
Oh, child molesters, child molesters.

01:19:32.200 --> 01:19:33.680
And he said, oh my God.

01:19:33.680 --> 01:19:35.760
Well, he said we could file a motion,

01:19:35.760 --> 01:19:38.640
but it'll be two years before we get a hearing.

01:19:38.640 --> 01:19:40.440
He said, you'll be home by then.

01:19:40.440 --> 01:19:41.280
I'm sorry, buddy.

01:19:41.280 --> 01:19:43.440
You're just gonna have to tough it out.

01:19:43.440 --> 01:19:46.880
And so I said to myself, you have lived

01:19:46.880 --> 01:19:51.040
in far worse places than Laredo, Pennsylvania.

01:19:51.040 --> 01:19:53.120
And I've gone nose to nose with men

01:19:53.120 --> 01:19:55.000
a lot tougher than the clowns

01:19:55.000 --> 01:19:57.120
that I ended up locked up with

01:19:57.120 --> 01:19:59.840
or being subordinate to.

01:19:59.840 --> 01:20:00.840
So.

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:03.120
So I decided I was just gonna take these lessons,

01:20:03.120 --> 01:20:05.040
these life lessons the CIA taught me

01:20:05.040 --> 01:20:09.320
and I was gonna use them to ensure that I remain safe

01:20:09.320 --> 01:20:12.240
and at the top of the social heap.

01:20:12.240 --> 01:20:14.600
And if that meant manipulating people,

01:20:14.600 --> 01:20:16.400
then I was gonna manipulate them.

01:20:16.400 --> 01:20:19.160
The book almost reads like a how-to

01:20:19.160 --> 01:20:22.320
of how to cope in really any environment.

01:20:22.320 --> 01:20:24.480
But you talk about the four different things

01:20:24.480 --> 01:20:26.000
that people respond to when you're trying

01:20:26.000 --> 01:20:27.400
to manipulate them to,

01:20:27.400 --> 01:20:29.600
I guess you're trying to recruit basically spies

01:20:29.600 --> 01:20:30.920
and people in informants.

01:20:30.920 --> 01:20:35.920
So there's revenge, greed, ideology, and excitement, right?

01:20:36.040 --> 01:20:39.160
And can you talk about what an example,

01:20:39.160 --> 01:20:44.160
like what's an example of how to get somebody to,

01:20:46.200 --> 01:20:49.680
for instance, steal something out of a CO's office

01:20:49.680 --> 01:20:51.840
or right like clips or something like that.

01:20:51.840 --> 01:20:53.920
Like how do you use those techniques

01:20:53.920 --> 01:20:56.360
to get somebody on your side?

01:20:56.360 --> 01:20:58.200
Well, let me preface this by saying that

01:20:58.200 --> 01:20:59.640
when I wrote the book,

01:20:59.640 --> 01:21:03.040
I asked Oliver Stone if he would write a blurb for me.

01:21:03.040 --> 01:21:04.720
I've known Oliver for a long time.

01:21:04.720 --> 01:21:06.240
He's a good guy.

01:21:06.240 --> 01:21:07.760
I consider us to be friends.

01:21:07.760 --> 01:21:09.520
He said, sure, I'll write a blurb.

01:21:09.520 --> 01:21:13.000
You notice that no blurb from Oliver appears on this book.

01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:17.320
So I sent it to him and we were coming up to deadlines.

01:21:17.320 --> 01:21:18.160
So I called him and I said,

01:21:18.160 --> 01:21:20.400
hey Oliver, I really need that blurb.

01:21:20.400 --> 01:21:21.840
Can you get it to me?

01:21:21.840 --> 01:21:25.120
And he kinda exhales.

01:21:25.120 --> 01:21:26.960
He said, all right, listen.

01:21:26.960 --> 01:21:28.240
I read this book.

01:21:28.240 --> 01:21:30.840
I think you come across as a total asshole.

01:21:31.800 --> 01:21:34.560
And I don't think I want my name associated with it.

01:21:36.720 --> 01:21:38.520
Can you pass me the book actually?

01:21:38.520 --> 01:21:42.920
So to give you an example of manipulating somebody

01:21:42.920 --> 01:21:43.800
to get something I need,

01:21:43.800 --> 01:21:47.240
you mentioned clips or these binder clips,

01:21:47.240 --> 01:21:48.800
these metal clips.

01:21:48.800 --> 01:21:49.880
Oh, that's okay, yeah.

01:21:49.880 --> 01:21:50.720
Right?

01:21:50.720 --> 01:21:52.080
Well, you're not allowed to have those in prison.

01:21:52.080 --> 01:21:54.120
They're considered to be contraband.

01:21:54.120 --> 01:21:56.240
But every once in a while you'll see one

01:21:56.240 --> 01:21:59.120
because somebody stole one out of the warden's office.

01:21:59.120 --> 01:22:01.720
Well, what you do is you identify the guy

01:22:01.720 --> 01:22:04.680
who mops the floor in the warden's office.

01:22:04.680 --> 01:22:05.980
I also needed highlighters,

01:22:05.980 --> 01:22:07.880
which are also considered contraband.

01:22:08.880 --> 01:22:11.920
And so you get close to this guy who mops the floor

01:22:11.920 --> 01:22:16.280
and you try to learn what's important to him.

01:22:16.280 --> 01:22:17.680
One of the things I learned in prison

01:22:17.680 --> 01:22:21.240
is everybody's innocent and everybody's appealing his case.

01:22:21.240 --> 01:22:22.080
Right?

01:22:22.080 --> 01:22:23.420
Even though they're all actually guilty

01:22:23.420 --> 01:22:24.660
and appealing their cases.

01:22:24.660 --> 01:22:25.500
Right.

01:22:25.500 --> 01:22:30.220
So you talk about how you were wronged

01:22:30.220 --> 01:22:31.820
by the justice system.

01:22:31.820 --> 01:22:33.420
And I bet you were wrong too.

01:22:33.420 --> 01:22:34.940
I bet they're trying to screw you

01:22:34.940 --> 01:22:37.220
so you can't appeal your case.

01:22:37.220 --> 01:22:38.740
You know what those bastards did to me?

01:22:38.740 --> 01:22:40.980
They took all my binders.

01:22:40.980 --> 01:22:43.620
I wish I could get back at them, you know?

01:22:43.620 --> 01:22:45.580
And the warden was talking smack about you

01:22:45.580 --> 01:22:46.420
the other day.

01:22:46.420 --> 01:22:47.900
I overheard him in the hall.

01:22:47.900 --> 01:22:49.820
And then you let him say,

01:22:49.820 --> 01:22:51.420
hey, you know what they have binders

01:22:51.420 --> 01:22:54.300
in the warden's office, I've seen them.

01:22:54.300 --> 01:22:55.220
Like it's his idea.

01:22:55.220 --> 01:22:56.060
Right, yeah, right.

01:22:56.060 --> 01:22:59.900
Oh man, I sure could use some of those binders

01:22:59.900 --> 01:23:03.420
and you know, we should get back at that bastard.

01:23:03.420 --> 01:23:04.740
The next thing you know,

01:23:04.740 --> 01:23:06.340
there's some binders laying on your pillow

01:23:06.340 --> 01:23:07.660
when you get back after lunch.

01:23:07.660 --> 01:23:10.580
Right, and you got a friend for the duration, right?

01:23:10.580 --> 01:23:11.420
Exactly.

01:23:11.420 --> 01:23:12.700
Yeah, exactly.

01:23:12.700 --> 01:23:14.500
You better not go back to jail

01:23:14.500 --> 01:23:16.740
because people are gonna know about this book.

01:23:16.740 --> 01:23:18.060
Yeah, they're gonna see it coming.

01:23:18.060 --> 01:23:20.500
They'll be smart about your, yeah.

01:23:20.540 --> 01:23:22.820
I'm surprised it's not banned in jail.

01:23:22.820 --> 01:23:23.660
Yeah.

01:23:23.660 --> 01:23:25.020
I'm almost offended that it's not.

01:23:25.020 --> 01:23:26.500
Yeah, I know, it should be, yeah.

01:23:26.500 --> 01:23:30.340
This whole thing happening now with Ukraine

01:23:30.340 --> 01:23:34.780
and the sort of dueling investigations

01:23:34.780 --> 01:23:37.620
that are dominating Washington right now,

01:23:37.620 --> 01:23:40.940
there's the Durham investigation on the one hand

01:23:40.940 --> 01:23:42.820
of the origins of Russiagate

01:23:42.820 --> 01:23:44.980
and all of your former colleagues

01:23:44.980 --> 01:23:47.740
are sort of heavily talking about that.

01:23:48.700 --> 01:23:51.940
And then there's the Ukraine situation

01:23:51.940 --> 01:23:53.780
with the whistleblower and impeachment and all that.

01:23:53.780 --> 01:23:55.140
How do you see all this playing out?

01:23:55.140 --> 01:23:57.180
What do you think is actually going on here?

01:23:57.180 --> 01:23:59.540
I mean, it's a very complicated tableau.

01:23:59.540 --> 01:24:01.300
It is complicated.

01:24:01.300 --> 01:24:05.180
I think Donald Trump probably did commit impeachable acts.

01:24:05.180 --> 01:24:08.180
I think that in public speeches,

01:24:08.180 --> 01:24:11.580
he has expressed willingness

01:24:11.580 --> 01:24:13.860
to commit other impeachable acts,

01:24:13.860 --> 01:24:17.700
like on Sunday when he said he wanted to take Iraq's oil

01:24:18.140 --> 01:24:20.580
or when he said that he wanted to bring back

01:24:20.580 --> 01:24:22.740
waterboarding and a hell of a lot worse.

01:24:23.660 --> 01:24:26.300
I think those are impeachable acts.

01:24:26.300 --> 01:24:29.260
I think that the Democrats, on the other hand,

01:24:29.260 --> 01:24:32.180
may be going about this whole thing the wrong way.

01:24:32.180 --> 01:24:33.820
I know that the rules were imposed

01:24:33.820 --> 01:24:37.180
by the Republican majority when they controlled the House,

01:24:37.180 --> 01:24:39.020
but that doesn't mean that closed sessions

01:24:39.020 --> 01:24:40.940
are the right thing to do.

01:24:40.940 --> 01:24:43.500
And so I think that it's to the benefit

01:24:43.500 --> 01:24:45.020
of the American people to have literally

01:24:45.020 --> 01:24:47.020
all of the information out there.

01:24:47.020 --> 01:24:49.340
I think that we deserve the information

01:24:49.340 --> 01:24:51.380
so that we can make our own judgments.

01:24:51.380 --> 01:24:53.620
But I think Donald Trump probably has put himself

01:24:53.620 --> 01:24:55.580
in a corner that he's not going to be able to get out of.

01:24:55.580 --> 01:24:56.700
You were an investigator

01:24:56.700 --> 01:24:58.180
for the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

01:24:58.180 --> 01:24:59.820
Is it normal?

01:24:59.820 --> 01:25:04.300
The thing, obviously, there's very often closed hearings,

01:25:04.300 --> 01:25:06.220
the things that go on in the skiff

01:25:06.220 --> 01:25:09.980
and the controlled, what does that stand for?

01:25:09.980 --> 01:25:11.300
I don't even remember.

01:25:11.300 --> 01:25:16.300
Secure, compartmentalized information.

01:25:17.060 --> 01:25:17.860
Facility, maybe?

01:25:17.860 --> 01:25:18.700
Facility, yeah.

01:25:20.700 --> 01:25:21.900
I guess the question is,

01:25:22.900 --> 01:25:24.500
is there anything that's odd about having

01:25:24.500 --> 01:25:27.700
an impeachment hearing out of the Intelligence Committee?

01:25:27.700 --> 01:25:30.140
Yeah, see, this is a big difference

01:25:30.140 --> 01:25:31.220
from the impeachment hearings

01:25:31.220 --> 01:25:34.660
of the Clinton administration and the Nixon administration.

01:25:34.660 --> 01:25:37.140
In those cases, there were joint committees.

01:25:37.140 --> 01:25:40.220
They were specifically impeachment committees,

01:25:40.220 --> 01:25:42.100
select committees.

01:25:42.100 --> 01:25:43.740
That's not the case now.

01:25:43.740 --> 01:25:46.260
Now, this is being handled by the Intelligence Committee,

01:25:46.260 --> 01:25:50.860
which by its very nature is going to be secret.

01:25:50.860 --> 01:25:52.340
And they're gonna have a lot of these hearings

01:25:52.340 --> 01:25:53.980
in the skiff behind closed doors

01:25:53.980 --> 01:25:56.980
with no access to journalists.

01:25:56.980 --> 01:26:00.620
I think that that's a disservice to the American people.

01:26:00.620 --> 01:26:02.100
We all remember things like,

01:26:02.100 --> 01:26:04.180
or maybe we don't remember them.

01:26:04.180 --> 01:26:08.020
Maybe we've seen video clips of Howard Baker

01:26:08.020 --> 01:26:09.940
asking a witness in the Watergate hearings,

01:26:09.940 --> 01:26:12.680
what did the president know and when did he know it?

01:26:12.680 --> 01:26:16.240
Well, we deserve to hear the answers to those questions.

01:26:16.240 --> 01:26:20.280
So yeah, I think that there should be a joint committee.

01:26:20.280 --> 01:26:22.200
If the joint committee, I'm sorry,

01:26:22.200 --> 01:26:25.200
if the Republicans don't wanna have their members

01:26:25.200 --> 01:26:27.520
serve in the joint committee, at the very least,

01:26:27.520 --> 01:26:29.480
there should be a select committee

01:26:29.480 --> 01:26:31.280
that meets in open session.

01:26:32.960 --> 01:26:35.280
Just a few more questions I'll ask you at once

01:26:35.280 --> 01:26:37.240
and then you can figure out which ones you wanna answer.

01:26:37.240 --> 01:26:40.320
First one is, did you join the Republican Party

01:26:40.320 --> 01:26:42.320
or did you just leave the Democratic Party?

01:26:43.320 --> 01:26:46.400
And then what happened to your pension?

01:26:46.400 --> 01:26:49.400
Did any Democrats defend you?

01:26:49.400 --> 01:26:51.520
And then this is kind of a long one,

01:26:51.520 --> 01:26:53.440
but Matt talks about in a piece he wrote

01:26:53.440 --> 01:26:56.160
about how people think that Donald Trump

01:26:56.160 --> 01:26:58.560
is like the worst thing that there is out there

01:26:58.560 --> 01:27:02.600
and that there's actually worse things

01:27:02.600 --> 01:27:04.240
that involves the intelligence community

01:27:04.240 --> 01:27:06.720
or the federal, and what is it federal?

01:27:07.640 --> 01:27:08.480
What do you call it?

01:27:08.480 --> 01:27:09.320
Federal bureaucracy.

01:27:09.320 --> 01:27:11.720
I was just really saying that they're potentially

01:27:11.720 --> 01:27:12.800
more dangerous than Trump is.

01:27:12.800 --> 01:27:15.880
Yes, which is why a coup isn't a good idea, for instance.

01:27:15.880 --> 01:27:18.600
So you as someone who's been in the CIA,

01:27:18.600 --> 01:27:20.120
can you offer any insight into that?

01:27:20.120 --> 01:27:21.200
Do you agree with that?

01:27:22.480 --> 01:27:23.320
And you don't have to.

01:27:23.320 --> 01:27:24.160
And you don't have to, but yeah,

01:27:24.160 --> 01:27:26.120
I thought that you would know

01:27:26.120 --> 01:27:27.080
what that would look like.

01:27:27.080 --> 01:27:29.640
Sure, I'm happy to answer all those questions.

01:27:29.640 --> 01:27:31.440
Number one, no, I'm not a Republican.

01:27:31.440 --> 01:27:33.680
I will never be a Republican.

01:27:33.680 --> 01:27:36.200
I can't be a Republican.

01:27:36.200 --> 01:27:40.000
I could see myself maybe, perhaps someday,

01:27:40.000 --> 01:27:43.720
being a Democrat again, but in the meantime,

01:27:43.720 --> 01:27:46.040
I consider myself to be part of

01:27:46.040 --> 01:27:48.320
what's called the Libertarian Left,

01:27:48.320 --> 01:27:50.160
and I'm comfortable with that.

01:27:52.120 --> 01:27:55.160
My pension, as part of my conviction,

